<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-ftc-part-two">
  <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part Two</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-ftc-part-two">
    <ul>
      <li><p>State the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus (Part 2) and use the evaluation notation <m>F(x)\Big|_a^b</m> to compute <m>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx</m> from any antiderivative <m>F</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Write the antiderivative rules for <m>e^x</m> and <m>\frac{1}{x}</m>, and their chain rule versions for <m>e^u u'</m> and <m>\frac{u'}{u}</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify <m>u</m> and <m>u'</m> in an integrand, factor out constant multiples, and apply the matching antiderivative rule.</p></li>
      <li><p>Check that the integrand is continuous on the interval of integration before applying the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now have enough antiderivative formulas to compute definite integrals exactly.
      Today we connect the accumulation functions we have been studying to a much faster way
      of evaluating integrals, and then practice the new antiderivative rules for the
      exponential and the logarithm.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-ftc2-facts">
    <title>Facts We Know</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            An accumulation function <m>A(x)=\int_c^x f(t)\,dt</m> can be used to calculate any
            definite integral:
            <m>\displaystyle \int_a^b f(t)\,dt=A(b)-A(a)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus guarantees that if <m>f(x)</m> is continuous,
            then <m>A(x)</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Any two antiderivatives <m>F(x)</m> and <m>G(x)</m> of <m>f(x)</m> differ by a
            constant on any interval where <m>f(x)</m> is continuous, meaning
            <m>G(x)=F(x)+C</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-ftc2-accumulation-example" workspace="0in">
    <title>An Accumulation Function in Action</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the accumulation function
        <m>\displaystyle A(x)=\int_1^x t\,dt</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-rate-function" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the function <m>f(t)</m> giving the rate of accumulation?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-initial-value" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is <m>A(1)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-find-accumulation" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>A(x)</m> by choosing the antiderivative of <m>f</m> that makes <m>A(1)</m>
          equal to the value you found.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-evaluate-with-A" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_0^3 x\,dx</m> using <m>A(x)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-evaluate-with-geometry" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_0^3 x\,dx</m> using the simpler accumulation formula
          we originally learned.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-why-same" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why do the last two tasks give the same answer? Use the facts about antiderivatives
          above to explain what happens to the constant <m>C</m> when you compute
          <m>A(3)-A(0)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-ftc-part-two">
    <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus (Part 2)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>F(x)</m> is <em>any</em> antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>f(x)</m> is
        continuous between <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, then
        <md>
          \int_a^b f(x)\,dx = F(b)-F(a)\text{.}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark xml:id="rem-ftc2-evaluation-notation">
    <title>Evaluation Notation</title>
    <p>
      The notation <m>F(x)\Big|_a^b</m> means <m>F(b)-F(a)</m>. With it, the theorem reads
      <m>\displaystyle \int_a^b f(x)\,dx=F(x)\Big|_a^b</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-ftc2-exp-log-rules" workspace="0in">
    <title>Antiderivative Rules for the Exponential and Logarithm</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Every derivative rule corresponds to an antiderivative rule. The statement
        <m>\frac{d}{dx}\big[F(x)\big]=f(x)</m> is equivalent to
        <m>\int f(x)\,dx=F(x)+C</m>. We have two new basic derivative rules:
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\big[e^x\big] \amp = e^x</mrow>
          <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\big[\ln(|x|)\big] \amp = \frac{1}{x}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-basic-rules" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write the integral statement that corresponds to each derivative rule.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-chain-rules" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The chain rule versions of these derivative rules are
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\big[e^u\big] \amp = e^u\cdot u'</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\big[\ln(|u|)\big] \amp = \frac{u'}{u}\text{.}</mrow>
          </md>
          Write the integral statement that corresponds to each one.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-ftc2-sum-constant-rules">
    <title>Sums and Constant Multiples</title>
    <p>
      We still have the sum and constant multiple rules:
      <md>
        \int c_1 f(x)+c_2 g(x)\,dx = c_1\int f(x)\,dx + c_2\int g(x)\,dx\text{.}
      </md>
      This means we can work on the individual terms of a <em>sum</em>, and we can factor out
      constant multiples.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-ftc2-sum-practice" workspace="0in">
    <title>Practice</title>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-practice-poly-recip" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle \int 2x^3+\frac{3}{x}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-practice-sqrt-exp" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle \int 4\sqrt{x}-2e^x\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-ftc2-chain-practice" workspace="0in">
    <title>Identifying <m>u</m> and <m>u'</m></title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The chain rule factor <m>u'</m> must be identified and present in the integrand to use
        the antiderivative rule. We can factor out constant multiples, but we cannot factor out
        formulas involving the variable. For each problem below:
        <ul>
          <li><p>identify what is likely to be <m>u</m> and calculate the required <m>u'</m>,</p></li>
          <li><p>factor out any constant multiples that are not part of <m>u'</m>, and</p></li>
          <li><p>use the antiderivative rule.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-chain-exp" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle \int 4e^{2x}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-chain-ln" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle \int \frac{6x}{x^2+4}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-chain-exp-constant" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>\displaystyle \int 2 e^{-3x} \,dx</m>. (Hint: Multiply and divide to get <m>u'</m>)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-ftc2-definite-integrals" workspace="0in">
    <title>Computing Definite Integrals with Antiderivatives</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now apply <xref ref="thm-ftc-part-two"/> by using your choice of antiderivative to
        compute each definite integral. Before you begin each one, verify that the interval in
        question does not include any discontinuities of the integrand.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-definite-poly-recip" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_1^3 2x^3+\frac{3}{x}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-continuity-check" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A student wants to evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_{-1}^{2}\frac{1}{x}\,dx</m> and
          writes <m>\ln|x|\Big|_{-1}^{2}=\ln(2)-\ln(1)=\ln(2)</m>. Check the continuity
          requirement of <xref ref="thm-ftc-part-two"/> on the interval <m>[-1,2]</m>. Is the
          student's computation justified? Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-definite-exp" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_0^2 4e^{2x}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-definite-ln" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_0^1 \frac{6x}{x^2+4}\,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-ftc2-definite-exp-const" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate <m>\displaystyle \int_0^4 2 e^{-3x} \,dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
